      children: "Confidence thresholds and deferrals: stop forcing the model to pretend it knows"
    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "If you require a binary decision 100% of the time, you're training everyone (including your system) to lie. Real-world decisioning has uncertainty: ambiguous cases exist, data is incomplete, and the cost of errors is asymmetric. A reliability-minded workflow includes deferrals - “send to human review”, “request more info”, “fall back to rules”, or “take the safe default”. This approach aligns with risk management: when uncertainty is high, reduce automation and increase oversight. In practice, you implement thresholds not as a single magic number, but as a policy that can vary by segment, risk appetite, and operational capacity. For example, you might auto-approve only when confidence is high and the potential harm is low, while routing borderline cases to review."
